exec
/bin/zsh -lc "nl -ba .scratch/photo-machine-capture/spec.md | sed -n '1,240p'; nl -ba .scratch/photo-machine-capture/issues/01-label-reading-core.md | sed -n '1,260p'; nl -ba .scratch/photo-machine-capture/issues/02-scan-ui.md | sed -n '1,260p'; nl -ba .scratch/photo-machine-capture/issues/03-verification.md | sed -n '1,240p'" in /Users/ericlee06/orca/projects/Health App
 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Photograph a machine's name plate to identify it
     2	
     3	Written 2026-08-11, at the user's request after milestone 3 merged. Not one of the numbered v1
     4	milestones — a feature that arrived from real gym use, which `docs/STATE.md` says outranks the
     5	backlog.
     6	
     7	## The ask, in the user's words
     8	
     9	> being able to take a picture of the machine name (when adding machine) and the app either
    10	> automatically finds the machine from the database or creates a new machine if it doesn't exist
    11	
    12	## What that means in this app's taxonomy
    13	
    14	Adding a machine already creates a `MachineInstance`. What the photo is really identifying is the
    15	**equipment model** — the catalog row (`EquipmentModel`) that names the hardware and links it to
    16	exercises. So:
    17	
    18	- **"finds the machine from the database"** → matches the name plate against the 1877 seeded
    19	  catalog models and fills in `Catalog model` on the New Machine sheet.
    20	- **"creates a new machine if it doesn't exist"** → creates a **user-created model**
    21	  (`isSeeded == false`, its own ID space per D4) prefilled with the manufacturer and model text
    22	  the camera read, and attaches it to the machine being added.
    23	
    24	The machine instance itself is created by the sheet the user is already in; the scan only fills
    25	in the two fields that are otherwise tedious to type at a gym: the model, and the label.
    26	
    27	## Decided up front (user, 2026-08-11)
    28	
    29	- **A scan never assigns a catalog identity on its own.** The sheet shows what the camera read
    30	  plus the ranked candidates, preselecting the top one only when the score, the manufacturer and
    31	  the margin over the runner-up all agree; one tap accepts it. → **D33**.
    32	  Rationale: D23 keys history, prefill and PRs on the model UUID, so a confidently-wrong match
    33	  silently splits the user's own history — the exact failure D4 was amended for. OCR on a scuffed,
    34	  angled, back-lit name plate misreads more than it looks like it will.
    35	- **The photo is read and discarded.** No image is persisted, so no schema change, no storage
    36	  growth, nothing new in the export or a future CloudKit sync. → **D34**.
    37	- Unmatched scans produce a **user-space** model, never a row that looks seeded. → **D35**.
    38	
    39	## How it works
    40	
    41	```
    42	[New Machine sheet] → "Scan label…"
    43	   ↓ camera (or photo library when there is no camera — Simulator, or a photo taken earlier)
    44	[Vision VNRecognizeTextRequest, on device, offline]
    45	   ↓ lines of text + confidence + relative height
    46	[CatalogMatcher — pure, IDF-weighted token scoring against the catalog]
    47	   ↓ ranked candidates
    48	[Result sheet]  ✓ top hit preselected (when unambiguous)  ·  alternatives  ·  "create new"
    49	   ↓
    50	model filled into the New Machine sheet (and the label defaulted, as picking a model already does)
    51	```
    52	
    53	### Reading the label (`Features/Gyms/MachineLabelOCR.swift`)
    54	
    55	Vision's `VNRecognizeTextRequest`, `.accurate`, language correction **off** — "Insignia",
    56	"Hammer Strength" and "Cybex" are not dictionary words and correction does more harm than good on
    57	them. Each line keeps its confidence and its height as a fraction of the image, because the model
    58	name is almost always the largest text on a name plate and the manufacturer sits above it.
    59	
    60	The photo's orientation tag is passed to Vision — a camera image is almost never `.up`, and both
    61	recognition quality and the top-line ordering depend on it. Library photos backed by a `CIImage`
    62	(HEIC, edited) are rendered rather than rejected.
    63	
    64	First-party, on-device, offline: no new dependency, and it works in a basement gym with no signal.
    65	It lives in `Features/Gyms` rather than `Domain/` because it orchestrates a platform framework and
    66	speaks `UIImage`; `LabelReading` and the matcher stay in `Domain`, testable without a camera.
    67	
    68	### Matching (`Domain/CatalogMatcher.swift`, pure)
    69	
    70	1. Normalise both sides: case-fold, strip diacritics and punctuation, collapse whitespace.
    71	2. Weight every token by **IDF across the catalog itself**, computed once when the index is built.
    72	   Single characters are tokens too: `4 Station` and `5 Station`, `R-4` and `R-6`, PRIME's `2:1`
    73	   and `4:1` are different machines, and dropping the digit made them indistinguishable. Lone
    74	   *letters* are dropped from the photo side only, where they are usually a bracket edge.
    75	   "series" and "press" appear in hundreds of rows and say little; "insignia", "sorinex" and
    76	   "vsl019bp" are nearly unique and say almost everything.
    77	3. Score each catalog row by the weighted share of its **model-name** tokens the reading covers,
    78	   allowing an edit distance of 1 (short tokens) or 2 (long tokens) so `Insigma`, `lnsignia` and
    79	   `INSIGNIA` all land on the same row. One recognised word may serve as evidence for exactly one
    80	   catalog word, and two words the catalog both knows are never treated as a misreading of each
    81	   other — "adduction" is not "abduction", it is the next machine along.
    82	4. Bonus when the row's manufacturer appears in the reading; small credit for explaining the
    83	   reading's own distinctive tokens (only small — name plates are full of "MAX 300 LB",
    84	   "READ MANUAL BEFORE USE" and serial numbers that no model name will ever explain).
    85	5. Return the top candidates above a floor, each with a 0–1 score the UI shows as a percentage.
    86	
    87	**Preselection needs three things at once**, because the first cross-review found four ways to be
    88	confidently wrong with only a score (`codex-review.md`):
    89	
    90	1. score ≥ **0.85**;
    91	2. the plate **names this row's manufacturer**, and names no other known one — an unknown or
    92	   contradicting brand cannot be waved through (a plate reading `NEWCO PENDULUM SQUAT` scored 0.97
    93	   against *Nautilus* Pendulum Squat before this);
    94	3. the runner-up is at least **0.08** behind — a near-tie is an ambiguity to show, not a coin flip
    95	   to resolve. Two machines in one photograph, or a short catalog name contained in a longer one,
    96	   both produce ties.
    97	
    98	Below **0.35** the sheet leads with the create-new *action*, not just different wording. Weak
    99	candidates are still listed above 0.25, labelled as weak.
   100	
   101	Scoring is `0.60 × name coverage + 0.30 × how much of the plate the row explains + 0.10 ×
   102	manufacturer named`, times 0.6 when the plate names a different known manufacturer. Words the
   103	catalog has never seen count as maximally distinctive rather than free — otherwise an unlisted
   104	brand costs a row nothing.
   105	
   106	### Creating a model from the reading
   107	
   108	When nothing matches, the reading is parsed into a manufacturer guess (a known catalog
   109	manufacturer found in the text wins, in its canonical spelling; otherwise the top line) and a
   110	model-name guess (the largest remaining line, junk lines dropped — load ratings, warnings, serial
   111	numbers). Both are editable before saving: the app proposes, the user decides.
   112	
   113	## Explicitly not in this feature
   114	
   115	- Recognising the *machine* (which of the three chest presses this is) — that is the user's label.
   116	- Reading weight stacks, plate counts or QR/serial codes.
   117	- Scanning from the logging flow (Add by Machine). Same core will serve it later if wanted.
   118	- Storing or exporting photos (D34).
   119	- Any network call. Nothing leaves the phone.
   120	
   121	## Tickets
   122	
   123	```
   124	01 label-reading-core  — Vision reader + pure matcher + model-name guessing, unit-tested
   125	└── 02 scan-ui         — "Scan label…" in the New Machine sheet, capture, results, create-new
   126	    └── 03 verification — XCUITest through a fixture image, docs, decisions D33–D35
   127	```
     1	# 01 — Label reading core: OCR, matcher, model-name guessing
     2	
     3	Status: resolved
     4	Blocked by: —
     5	
     6	All the logic, none of the UI. The matcher is pure and gets the hard tests; the Vision wrapper is
     7	thin enough to trust once an end-to-end test proves it reads a rendered label.
     8	
     9	## Files
    10	
    11	- `WorkoutTracker/Domain/MachineLabelText.swift` — `LabelReading` (lines + confidence + relative
    12	  height), normalisation, tokenisation, and the manufacturer/model-name guess. Pure.
    13	- `WorkoutTracker/Domain/CatalogMatcher.swift` — `CatalogMatchIndex` (built once from the catalog,
    14	  carries IDF weights) and the ranking. Pure.
    15	- `WorkoutTracker/Domain/MachineLabelOCR.swift` — `VNRecognizeTextRequest` wrapper returning a
    16	  `LabelReading`. Imports Vision; imports no UI.
    17	- `WorkoutTrackerTests/CatalogMatcherTests.swift`, `WorkoutTrackerTests/MachineLabelOCRTests.swift`
    18	
    19	## Acceptance criteria
    20	
    21	- [x] Matching is **pure and deterministic**: same reading + same index → same ranking, ties broken
    22	      totally (score, then name, then id).
    23	- [x] Real catalog rows are found from realistic readings: all-caps plates, the manufacturer on its
    24	      own line, line order scrambled, and single-character OCR errors (`Insigma`, `lnsignia`,
    25	      `HAMIER STRENGTH`).
    26	- [x] Junk on the plate does not sink the match: `MAX 300 LB`, `READ MANUAL BEFORE USE`, serial
    27	      numbers, and duplicated warning text.
    28	- [x] A plate for hardware the catalog does not have scores **below the create-new floor** — a
    29	      confident wrong answer is worse than no answer (D33/D4).
    30	- [x] Two different models from the same manufacturer (Insignia Chest Press vs. Insignia Shoulder
    31	      Press) rank in the right order; the loser still appears as an alternative.
    32	- [x] IDF weighting is real: a reading of only common words ("SERIES PRESS") matches nothing
    33	      confidently.
    34	- [x] Index build over the full 1877-row seeded catalog, plus a ranking, completes fast enough to
    35	      run inline on a tap (assert a loose bound; the point is catching an accidental O(n²)).
    36	- [x] The manufacturer guess returns the catalog's **canonical spelling** when the plate names a
    37	      known manufacturer, and the model guess drops junk lines.
    38	- [x] `MachineLabelOCR` reads a label rendered in the test itself (Core Graphics → `CGImage`) and
    39	      the matcher picks the right catalog row from it — the one test that proves the whole chain.
    40	- [x] No UI import in `Domain/`; no network call anywhere.
    41	
    42	## Resolution (2026-08-11)
    43	
    44	`Domain/MachineLabelText.swift` (reading value type, normalisation, junk-line and name guessing),
    45	`Domain/CatalogMatcher.swift` (IDF-weighted index + ranking), `Domain/MachineLabelOCR.swift`
    46	(Vision). Tests: `CatalogMatcherTests` (21 cases) and `MachineLabelOCRTests`, which renders a name
    47	plate with Core Graphics and runs the whole chain — pixels → Vision → matcher → catalog row.
    48	
    49	Two things worth knowing:
    50	
    51	- **Coverage is measured over the model name, not the whole row.** Counting manufacturer tokens
    52	  in the denominator let "Insignia Series Row" score 86% on a plate that plainly said Chest Press,
    53	  purely because it shared "Life Fitness Insignia Series". Name-only coverage plus a separate
    54	  brand bonus puts the right row at 100% and its stablemates at 75–79%.
    55	- **The confident bar is 0.85, above that stablemate band** — deliberately, so a misread of the one
    56	  distinguishing word cannot preselect a sibling. `onlyTheRightRowClearsTheConfidentBarAmongItsStablemates`
    57	  locks that in against the real 1877-row catalog.
    58	
    59	## Codex cross-review round 1 (2026-08-11)
    60	
    61	`codex-review.md` — verdict "do not merge", four **critical** ways to preselect a confidently
    62	wrong catalog UUID, all reproduced against the shipped 1877-row catalog and all fixed here:
    63	
    64	1. `NEWCO PENDULUM SQUAT` scored **0.97** against *Nautilus* Pendulum Squat — words the catalog
    65	   has never seen weighed nothing. Unseen words now count as maximally distinctive, a plate that
    66	   names a *different* known brand costs the row 40%, and preselection requires the row's own
    67	   manufacturer to be named.
    68	2. `NAUTILUS 5 STATION` tied 4/5/9 Station at 1.00 — the tokenizer dropped single characters.
    69	   Catalog tokens now keep everything (`R-4` vs `R-6`, PRIME `2:1` vs `4:1`); only the *photo*
    70	   side drops lone letters. A test asserts no two shipped rows share a matcher identity.
    71	3. A short generic row (`Back Extension`) tied the longer specific one it is contained in, and a
    72	   photo of two machines tied both. Explanation of the plate's own evidence now carries 30% of the
    73	   score, and preselection needs 0.08 of daylight over the runner-up.
    74	4. One recognised word could be evidence for two catalog words — "abduction" fuzzy-covering
    75	   "adduction". Fuzzy assignment is now one-to-one, and two words the catalog both knows are never
    76	   treated as a misreading of each other.
    77	
    78	Also fixed: non-deterministic scores from summing an unordered `Set` of floats (findings 7),
    79	and the brand guess searching the whole photo instead of the brand line (finding 9).
    80	`CatalogMatcherAdversarialTests` replays every case above against the real catalog.
     1	# 02 — Scan UI: "Scan label…" in the New Machine sheet
     2	
     3	Status: resolved
     4	Blocked by: 01
     5	
     6	## Files
     7	
     8	- `WorkoutTracker/Features/Gyms/ScanMachineLabelSheet.swift` — capture → reading → candidates.
     9	- `WorkoutTracker/Features/Gyms/ImagePicker.swift` — `UIImagePickerController` representable
    10	  (camera, or photo library when there is no camera).
    11	- `WorkoutTracker/Features/Gyms/GymsView.swift` — the "Scan label…" row in `MachineEditorSheet`,
    12	  and prefill parameters on `AddModelSheet`.
    13	- `INFOPLIST_KEY_NSCameraUsageDescription` in the app target's build settings (the project
    14	  generates its Info.plist from these keys — this is the project's own idiom, not file
    15	  registration, which stays out of `project.pbxproj` per CLAUDE.md).
    16	
    17	## Behavior
    18	
    19	- New Machine sheet grows one row under Catalog model: **Scan label…** (camera icon).
    20	- Camera when the device has one; otherwise straight to the photo library, with the reason stated
    21	  rather than a dead button. A photo taken earlier is a legitimate input.
    22	- While Vision runs: a progress state, no frozen sheet.
    23	- Result: the recognised text, then candidates ranked with percentages, the top one preselected
    24	  when it clears the confident bar (D33 — preselected, never auto-applied). Actions: **Use This**
    25	  and **None of these — create new**.
    26	- Create-new opens the existing New Model sheet **prefilled** with the manufacturer and model
    27	  guesses, still fully editable, and links it to the machine on save.
    28	- Nothing readable in the photo → say so plainly and offer create-new (with the raw text, if any)
    29	  or retake. Never a silent dismissal.
    30	- The image is released as soon as the reading is produced (D34).
    31	
    32	## Acceptance criteria
    33	
    34	- [x] Accessibility identifiers: `scanMachineLabel`, `scanUseCandidate`, `scanCreateNew`,
    35	      `scanCandidate.<modelName>`, `scanReadingText`, `scanStatus`.
    36	- [x] Accepting a candidate fills Catalog model in the editor and defaults the label exactly as
    37	      picking a model by hand already does (D3) — no second code path for naming.
    38	- [x] Camera permission denied, no camera, and an unreadable photo are all handled with a stated
    39	      reason and a way forward.
    40	- [x] No photo is written to disk or into the store (D34).
    41	- [x] Vision runs off the main actor; the sheet stays responsive.
    42	- [x] A launch argument (`-uiTestScanFixture`) substitutes a rendered fixture label for the
    43	      camera, so ticket 03 can drive the flow on a Simulator that has no camera. Test-only path,
    44	      gated exactly like `-uiTestReset`.
    45	
    46	## Resolution (2026-08-11)
    47	
    48	`Features/Gyms/ScanMachineLabelSheet.swift` and `ImagePicker.swift`; `MachineEditorSheet` grew a
    49	"Scan label…" row and `AddModelSheet` grew prefill parameters. Camera and photo-library usage
    50	strings added as `INFOPLIST_KEY_*` build settings (the project generates its Info.plist, so this
    51	is its own idiom — no file registration touched).
    52	
    53	Accepting a scanned model assigns the same `model` state a hand-picked one does, so the D3 label
    54	default has exactly one implementation. `-uiTestScanFixture` renders a name plate in place of the
    55	camera, because the Simulator has none and the flow was otherwise undrivable by XCUITest.
    56	
    57	## Codex cross-review round 1 (2026-08-11)
    58	
    59	Fixed here: orientation is passed to Vision and `CIImage`-backed photos are rendered rather than
    60	called unreadable (finding 5); camera availability *and* authorization are resolved before
    61	anything is presented, with the reason stated and Settings / photo-library / by-hand routes
    62	offered (finding 6); below the create-new floor the create-new **action** leads rather than just
    63	the wording (finding 8); and the Vision adapter moved to `Features/Gyms`, since `Domain/` is value
    64	types and pure logic (finding 12).
     1	# 03 — Verification and documentation
     2	
     3	Status: resolved
     4	Blocked by: 01, 02
     5	
     6	## Acceptance criteria
     7	
     8	- [x] One XCUITest: New Machine → Scan label… → (fixture image) → the right catalog model is
     9	      preselected → Use This → the machine saves with that model attached. One test; the UI suite
    10	      already runs ~7.5 minutes.
    11	- [x] A second, cheap assertion in the same test: "None of these — create new" reaches the New
    12	      Model sheet with the manufacturer and model fields prefilled.
    13	- [x] `docs/DECISIONS.md`: D33 (a scan proposes, the user confirms), D34 (photos are read and
    14	      discarded), D35 (unmatched scans create user-space models, never seeded-looking rows).
    15	- [x] `docs/SPEC.md`: the taxonomy/recording sections say scanning exists and what it can and
    16	      cannot identify.
    17	- [x] `docs/STATE.md`: status, test counts, and the camera-permission gotcha (a missing usage
    18	      string is a crash, not a prompt).
    19	- [x] Full suite green, re-run independently.
    20	- [x] Codex cross-review (T6) before merge, with the review prompt aimed at the matcher's failure
    21	      modes — a wrong-but-confident match is the defect that matters here.
    22	
    23	## Resolution (2026-08-11)
    24	
    25	`WorkoutTrackerUITests/ScanMachineLabelUITests.swift` — two tests: scan → confident row
    26	preselected → Use This → machine saved under the scanned name; and scan → create-new → New Model
    27	sheet prefilled with "Life Fitness" / "Insignia Series Chest Press".
    28	
    29	Decisions D33–D35 recorded; SPEC taxonomy notes scanning and what it cannot identify.
    30	Suite: **272 unit + 12 UI tests**.
    31	
    32	## Codex cross-review round 1 (2026-08-11)
    33	
    34	Verdict "do not merge" — four critical matcher defects, all real, all fixed (see tickets 01/02).
    35	Test gaps the review named are closed: `CatalogMatcherAdversarialTests` runs the negatives against
    36	the shipped catalog instead of a six-row fixture, `MachineLabelOCRTests` covers rotated and
    37	`CIImage`-backed photos, and the UI test now asserts the persisted machine→model *relationship*
    38	(the row subtitle is `machine.model?.displayName`), not just the defaulted label.
    39	
    40	Suite after the fixes: **284 unit + 12 UI tests**.

