docs/SPEC.md:27:- Adding a machine can **photograph its name plate** (D33–D35): Vision reads the text on device, the app ranks catalog models against it and offers the candidates with scores, and the user confirms one — or creates a user-space model prefilled from the reading. The photo is discarded after reading; nothing leaves the phone. A scan identifies the *model*, never which of the gym's three chest presses this is — that is the machine's label, which the user writes.
docs/DECISIONS.md:41:| D33 | **A scanned name plate proposes a catalog model; the user confirms it.** The sheet shows the recognised text and the ranked candidates with scores, and always offers "none of these". Nothing is applied without a tap. A row is **preselected only when all three hold**: score ≥ 0.85, the plate names that row's manufacturer and no other known one, and the runner-up is ≥ 0.08 behind. Amended 2026-08-11 after the cross-review, which found four ways to preselect a wrong row on a score alone. | D23 keys history, prefill and PRs on the model UUID, so a confident wrong match splits the user's own history — the same damage duplicate catalog identities did (codex-review-4). Real plates are scuffed, angled and back-lit, and stablemates share most of their name: a Life Fitness Insignia chest press scores 100% while five siblings sit at 75–79%, so the *distinguishing* word is often the one a camera misses. |
docs/DECISIONS.md:42:| D34 | **A scan reads the photo and discards it.** No image is written to the store or to disk; only the recognised text (transient) and the model the user picks survive. | Keeping images would mean a schema change, storage that grows with every machine, a decision about whether exports carry pixels, and a CloudKit asset story — all to solve a problem the user did not have. Recognition is on-device (Vision), so no photograph of the user's gym leaves the phone. |
docs/DECISIONS.md:43:| D35 | **An unmatched scan creates a *user-space* model** (`isSeeded == false`), prefilled with the manufacturer in the catalog's canonical spelling when the brand is known, and the plate's most prominent non-junk line as the model name. Both fields stay editable before saving. | D4: a plausible-but-invented catalog row is worse than an absent one, so OCR output must never masquerade as seeded data. Canonical brand spelling keeps `LIFE FITNESS` from sorting as a different manufacturer than the 200 seeded `Life Fitness` rows, which is a real cost with no upside. |
docs/STATE.md:19:**Machine-label scanning is built on branch `photo-machine-capture`** (uncommitted): photograph a
docs/STATE.md:22:(D33–D35, `.scratch/photo-machine-capture/`). Suite: **272 unit + 12 UI tests**.
docs/STATE.md:47:   `photo-machine-capture`. The matcher's thresholds (0.85 confident / 0.35 create-new) are tuned
docs/STATE.md:48:   against test fixtures and one rendered plate — the first real gym photos are the evidence that
docs/STATE.md:81:  all — hence `-uiTestScanFixture`, which renders a name plate in place of the picker. And a
.scratch/photo-machine-capture/issues/03-verification.md:8:- [x] One XCUITest: New Machine → Scan label… → (fixture image) → the right catalog model is
.scratch/photo-machine-capture/issues/03-verification.md:13:- [x] `docs/DECISIONS.md`: D33 (a scan proposes, the user confirms), D34 (photos are read and
.scratch/photo-machine-capture/issues/03-verification.md:14:      discarded), D35 (unmatched scans create user-space models, never seeded-looking rows).
.scratch/photo-machine-capture/issues/03-verification.md:25:`WorkoutTrackerUITests/ScanMachineLabelUITests.swift` — two tests: scan → confident row
